Issue #92 is still not done. No tacplus build has run since the new looper image went live, and I could not record this run's wait, because the looper's own checkpoint tool failed.

**Where it stands**
- The new image is live in tacplus-dev/build. The security report for cloud a6cdaac (job https://gitlab.com/tacplus-dev/build/-/jobs/18342552507) has the `### Durations (looper#207)` section: "The scanners: 5s, 3 at a time; one after another, 7s." So the scanners now run side by side.
- The issue is only done when a **tacplus** `security-tacplus` job takes well under 30 minutes (it took 56 before). tacplus main's last commits are still the HISTORY.md-only ones from 2026-11-24 15:24Z, so no such job has run yet. GitLab's API won't let this run read tacplus jobs, and no member has commented since the last run.
- The push that woke this run (71d3931..a6cdaac, #107, the looper pool) has nothing to do with this issue. The checkout is clean, so there was nothing to commit or land.

**The failure:** `repo-looper --wait-until 2026-11-25T12:00:00Z …` failed twice with `/builds/looper/cloud/.checkpoint.<cksum>.lock: Permission denied`. The tool tries to create its lock file in `/builds/looper/cloud`, which is owned by root and not writable by my user, looper-92. As a result the `looper/cloud-92` branch still holds the previous run's checkpoint, which says the same thing. This run's wait until 12:00Z is not recorded, so the issue may back off on the looper's default instead. This is a problem in the looper's environment, not in cloud, and I can't fix it from here.

**What's left:** a member comments the URL of a tacplus `security-tacplus` job that ran on the new image (`790b7849…`), or its `### Durations`. The next run then checks it is well under 30 minutes, comments the result and closes #92.

No handlers were touched, so the trace rule (rule 13) doesn't apply.
